                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the credibility gap`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Two finalist dossiers are on the table: <strong>Dr Priya Anand</strong>, a top-decile interventional cardiologist with deep clinical credibility but no formal management experience; and <strong>Marcus Reeve</strong>, a seasoned NHS general manager with turnaround credentials but no clinical background. Before you choose, diagnose <em>what kind of credibility this unit actually needs</em>. Goodall's expert-leadership theory says credibility in an expert unit comes from inherent domain knowledge and the trust of the people being led — and here the PI-of-record role and the registry data both require a credentialed cardiologist. Map where consultant trust, commissioner confidence and trials eligibility each come from, then set a provisional lean.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Select & structure the appointment`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Make the hire — but the real decision is rarely binary. You can appoint one finalist and pair them with the other: an <strong>expert leader with a general-manager deputy</strong> captures both credibility and managerial bandwidth. Define the decision rights and allocate the <strong>£350,000</strong> transition budget across protected clinical sessions, the deputy/COO hire, leadership coaching and a consultant-retention package. Remember the four flight-risk consultants: each costs <em>£180,000 and nine months</em> to replace, and without them the registry data has no one to author. Spend on the figurehead alone and you protect the name, not the asset.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Respond to the performance crisis`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Week 8, and the chosen leader hits a wall. Two unit consultants <strong>formally object</strong> to the appointment, one complex case has a <strong>poor outcome</strong> that threatens the registry submission, and a commissioner <strong>requests a meeting</strong>. The board must decide how to back — or constrain — the leader. Visible sponsorship reinforces their standing; stepping in to "rescue" it publicly strips their authority and tells the unit the leader has no real mandate. Goodall's question: <em>did you sponsor the leader's credibility, or replace it?</em> Protect both the submission and the leader's standing on day 60.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the outcome to stakeholders`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The 15 May window closes. Present the board's defence to the lead NHS commissioner and the consultant body. Justify the <strong>appointment model</strong>, the credibility built and the projected impact on outcomes, retention and the registry ranking — and show how the choice holds over <em>18 months</em>, not just past the deadline. Defend the figurehead instead of the structure, or over-promise a top-decile ranking you can't evidence, and the commissioner will test it. Lead with the KPI that actually drives the rest: leader credibility.`,

                    r1_source_label:        `Where does this unit's credibility actually come from? Name the decisive source.`,
                    r1_lean_label:          `Provisional lean, with Goodall's expert-leadership theory in mind.`,
