                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_clinical:      `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> In this unit credibility is clinical: the consultants follow a leader they believe reads interventional data, the trials role needs a credentialed PI, and the registry numbers need an author the commissioners trust. A non-clinician could not acquire that inside 14 weeks. Now let the lean follow the diagnosis.`,
                    coach_r1_admin:         `<strong>Management polish is not the unit's credibility.</strong> Yes, the rota and the budget need running — but the consultants don't grant a mandate to someone who can't defend the registry numbers in the room. You've named the second-order problem, not the decisive source.`,
                    coach_r1_generic:       `<strong>“A generic good leader” is exactly the trap.</strong> Goodall's research is that in expert organisations the specifics of the work are the credibility. Vision and presence won't author the registry data or hold the cath-lab room. Diagnose where trust actually comes from before you shortlist a profile.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp board diagnosis.</strong> You named the credibility source as clinical AND leaned to the expert — the Goodall default in an expert unit. That gives you a candidate the consultants, the commissioners and the trials sponsors can all believe in. The open question is now structure, not identity.`,
                    coach_r1_generalistlean:`<strong>Leaning to the generalist this early is a bet against your own unit.</strong> Management capability is portable, but it is contextual credibility that commands clinicians. You can supply management alongside an expert — you can't easily supply credibility alongside a manager. Re-examine the lean.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>You're shortlisting on instinct.</strong> Without an evidenced credibility-requirements map you can't say whether the unit needs clinical authority or managerial bandwidth most — and that is the whole decision. A thin diagnosis is how boards talk themselves into the comfortable hire.`,
                    coach_r2_expertdeputy:  `<strong>That's the structural answer to a false binary.</strong> An expert Director carries the credibility the consultants and commissioners require; a general-manager deputy supplies the rota, budget and commissioner bandwidth. Goodall's point exactly: credibility present, management supplied. Now make sure the decision rights and the budget back it up.`,
                    coach_r2_expertsolo:    `<strong>You gave Dr Anand the whole unit and no manager.</strong> Six weeks in, the rota is broken, the trials budget is overspent, and she's back in the cath lab because that's where she's comfortable. Expertise got you the room's trust — but who is running the operation while she operates? Where in your £350,000 did you fund management bandwidth?`,
                    coach_r2_generalistsolo:`<strong>You appointed Marcus for “manageability”.</strong> Day three he chairs the consultant meeting and is asked how he'll defend the registry numbers to commissioners. He doesn't read interventional data and the room knows it. Two consultants email the chair that night. Credibility precedes mandate — what does Marcus actually have the authority to decide here?`,
                    coach_r2_generalistdeputy:`<strong>A clinician advising a manager is not the same as a clinician with authority.</strong> If final clinical and registry calls sit with a non-clinician, the consultants still don't have a leader who can defend their data. The deputy structure is right; you've put the wrong person on top of it.`,
                    coach_r2_rightsmanager: `<strong>Final clinical authority with a non-clinician undoes the credibility you just bought.</strong> Whoever defends the registry numbers needs the standing to make the clinical and registry calls. Hand that to the manager and the consultants know the clinician is decorative.`,
                    coach_r2_rightsblurred: `<strong>Vague decision rights are how the rota and the registry both stall.</strong> “They'll work it out” means nobody owns the clinical call under pressure. Clarity of authority is part of credibility — name who decides what before week 8 forces the question.`,
                    coach_r2_nodeputy:      `<strong>An expert leader with no deputy and no management money is the rota waiting to break.</strong> You protected the credibility and starved the bandwidth. Expertise needs a complementary structure — fund the deputy/COO, or your star clinician ends up running spreadsheets instead of the cath lab.`,
                    coach_r2_noretention:   `<strong>You spent the budget on the leader and nothing on the team.</strong> Four consultants signalled they might leave; each is £180,000 and nine months to replace. If three follow Voss, the registry data has no one to author — you protected the figurehead, not the asset. Fund the retention package.`,
                    coach_r3_sponsor:       `<strong>That's sponsorship, not interference.</strong> Publicly affirming the leader's authority, taking them into the commissioner meeting and routing the objections through them reinforces the standing the consultants need to see. The board backed the leader's credibility instead of replacing it.`,
                    coach_r3_rescue:        `<strong>The board stepped in and rescued it — and just told the unit the leader has no real authority.</strong> You overruled them in front of the commissioner and the consultants. Goodall would ask: did you sponsor the leader's credibility or replace it? What's left of their standing on day 60?`,
                    coach_r3_distance:      `<strong>Leaving the leader to “sink or swim” at week 8 is abandonment dressed as a test.</strong> A new leader under a coordinated objection needs visible board backing to survive — distance reads to the consultants as a board that already regrets the hire. The standing erodes by default.`,
                    coach_r3_hide:          `<strong>Managing the poor-outcome case out of the submission is how the registry credibility actually dies.</strong> If the commissioner finds an unexplained case, the data is worth nothing and the re-tender clause triggers. The credentialed leader authoring an honest clinical review is the only thing that holds the numbers.`,
                    coach_r3_audit:         `<strong>An external audit over the leader's head says the board doesn't trust its own appointment.</strong> If you wanted a credible leader to stand behind the data, let them author the review — commissioning an audit around them tells the consultants the leader is on probation, not in charge.`,
                    coach_r3_appease:       `<strong>Promising the objectors the leader will be “kept in check” is undermining dressed as peacemaking.</strong> You bought a quiet week and spent the leader's authority to do it. The two dissenters now know the board will roll the leader under pressure — that's a recurring problem, not a settled one.`,
                    coach_r4_model:         `<strong>Defending the model is the durable defence.</strong> Credibility plus supplied management bandwidth, with evidenced projections across performance, retention and ranking, is a story the commissioner and the consultants can both check. You're defending a structure, not a personality.`,
                    coach_r4_figurehead:    `<strong>Defending the name without the structure is thin.</strong> Reputation got you in the room; it doesn't answer how the rota runs or how the registry data holds for 18 months. The commissioner is asking about the system you built, not the leader's CV.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Claiming the submission “was always safe” and hiding the retention and the crisis case is exactly what I'll probe.</strong> If you won't break out who you kept and how the poor-outcome case was handled, I read it as a story that doesn't survive contact. Spin invites the look it's trying to avoid.`,
                    coach_r4_durable:       `<strong>That holds up at 18 months.</strong> Defending the appointment model on credibility, retention and ranking — with projections beyond 15 May, not just to it — is the board statement that survives the commissioner and the consultant body. You built a structure designed to last, and you defended it as one.`,
                    coach_r4_window:        `<strong>Surviving 15 May is not the same as a durable appointment.</strong> If the defence stops at the window, the consultants and I both notice you have no answer for month six. The point was an 18-month structure, not a one-deadline rescue.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>Guaranteeing a top-decile ranking and zero consultant losses is a future liability, not reassurance.</strong> You can't evidence either, and when one consultant leaves the whole defence looks dishonest. Commit to what the structure can actually deliver.`,
                    coach_r4_cost:          `<strong>Leading with “we spent the £350k frugally” misreads the room.</strong> The commissioner isn't worried you overspent £350k — they're worried about £41M of activity and a £12M re-tender. Frugality is not the KPI that defends this appointment.`,

