                    insight_credible:       `Credible & durable: you solved the appointment as a system — an expert leader the consultants believe in, a general-manager deputy supplying the bandwidth, a funded retention package, and visible board sponsorship through the crisis. The registry data has a credible author, the re-tender clause is off the table, and the model holds beyond the window.`,
                    insight_collapse:       `Credibility collapse: the unit ended without a leader the room believes in. Either the generalist never earned the mandate, the expert was left to run an operation alone, or the board stripped the leader's authority in the crisis. With credibility gone, performance, retention and the registry data all follow it down.`,
                    insight_short:          `A mixed appointment. You got parts of it right but left a gap — credibility, structure, retention or the crisis response didn't fully line up, and the commissioner and the consultants can still feel the seam.`,
                    insight_quartile:       `Unit performance slipped below the top-quartile threshold (70). A registry-ranking drop below the top quartile is exactly what triggers the £12M outcome-contingent re-tender — the performance line was never just a number.`,
                    insight_retention:      `You lost ground on consultant retention. Each departure is £180,000 and nine months to replace, and the flight-risk consultants are the ones who author the registry data. Protecting the leader while the team walks protects the figurehead, not the asset.`,
                    insight_team:           `You held the team: nearly all eleven unit consultants stayed. The funded retention package kept the people who actually author the registry data — leader credibility and team retention rose together, exactly as Goodall predicts.`,
                    insight_overintervene:  `The board over-intervened in the crisis. Rescuing the situation publicly stripped the new leader's authority — you sponsored nothing and replaced everything. The standing you spent £350k to build did not survive the rescue.`,
                    insight_overpromise:    `You over-promised in the defence. A guaranteed top-decile ranking and zero losses you can't evidence is a liability the commissioner will hold you to — durable credibility is built on commitments you can keep, not the ones that reassure fastest.`,
                    insight_trusted:        `Leader credibility is high and risk is low — the appointment is now believed rather than merely announced. That is the credibility-as-prerequisite insight made real: with the room's trust, the mandate actually functions.`,

                    hint_overintervene:     `The board rescued the crisis and stripped the leader's authority. Sponsorship reinforces standing; intervention replaces it — back the leader visibly, don't speak over them.`,
                    hint_overpromise:       `You've guaranteed outcomes you can't evidence. Pull back to what the structure can actually deliver — over-promising is a liability the commissioner will test.`,
                    hint_retention:         `Consultant retention is dangerously low. The flight-risk consultants author the registry data — fund the retention package, or the leader inherits a unit with no one to write the numbers.`,
                    hint_cred:              `Leader credibility has collapsed. In an expert unit the mandate doesn't function without it — re-examine the appointment, the decision rights and the board's backing.`,
                    hint_risk:              `Risk exposure is dangerously high — the re-tender, talent-flight and registry-failure risks are compounding. Restore credibility, hold the team, and let the leader author the data.`,
                    hint_quartile:          `Unit performance is below the top-quartile threshold with little time left. A drop below top quartile triggers the £12M re-tender — fund the management bandwidth and protect the registry submission.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — credibility high, performance above the quartile line, risk low. Defend the model on credibility over 18 months to lock it in.`,

